Four gaps, all confirmed by reading the tree rather than assumed.

1. FLOOR-NOT-ORACLE. The README said what the gate does and never what a
   green check is NOT evidence of. New section carries the measured record
   from docs/dogfood-log.md. Three structural reasons — tests authored beside
   their implementation pass by construction, a check cannot observe a
   dependence on the environment it is, and the cheapest way to go green is
   not always the correct fix. Framed as what a check-command gate IS, not as
   a defect.

2. DOGFOOD LOG WAS UNREACHABLE from the front page. Now linked first in Docs,
   described honestly. Known-issue #6 already resolved the outsider-opacity
   objection that kept it unlinked.

3. TIERED CHECKS. Fast subset at Stop / full suite in CI, split by what each
   tier can OBSERVE rather than by speed, plus the credential-stripping CI
   step as its own step and why it must be separate.

4. COST MODEL. "Paid over and over" overstated it. Arming is per-session and
   resets on a clean accept, so a long turn editing twenty files pays for one
   check.

EVERY NUMBER RE-DERIVED FROM THE LOG, not from memory:
- 13 cycles on the current kernel in THIS repo (9 at 08-11 + 2 + 2 at 08-12).
  An earlier draft said ~22, which pooled cc-api-daemon's 9 into it — and
  dogfood-log.md:468 says "Do NOT pool this stream with the kkamak-on-kkamak
  stream." It also double-counted, since the next sentence describes those
  same 9 separately. Corrected, and the second stream is now named as
  separate.
- zero real defects / one false positive: consistent across all three
  rewritten-kernel entries (lines 70, 147, 432).
- 319 pre-existing tests unbroken by the save() signature change: line 368.
- 9 of 9 accepted with CI red on three: line 585.

Suite unchanged: 712 pass / 3 skip / 0 fail. packaging.test.ts pins the
README and still passes.

NOT PUSHED: publishing measured counts on a public project's front page is
the maintainer's call, and it is pending.

